1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,310
This week,

2
00:00:01,350 --> 00:00:04,170
Microsoft offered buyouts to about 60,000

3
00:00:03,970 --> 00:00:04,750
employees.

4
00:00:05,290 --> 00:00:06,870
That's roughly a quarter of its

5
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:07,690
entire workforce.

6
00:00:08,587 --> 00:00:10,427
Headlines are full of layoffs and

7
00:00:10,227 --> 00:00:11,487
artificial intelligence.

8
00:00:11,807 --> 00:00:12,167
So,

9
00:00:12,207 --> 00:00:13,347
is your job in danger?

10
00:00:13,891 --> 00:00:15,411
Let's skip the panic and look

11
00:00:15,211 --> 00:00:16,691
at the government's own numbers,

12
00:00:16,491 --> 00:00:18,251
because they tell a very different

13
00:00:18,051 --> 00:00:19,131
story than the headlines.

14
00:00:20,006 --> 00:00:21,106
And stick around,

15
00:00:21,206 --> 00:00:23,186
because one industry is still hiring

16
00:00:22,986 --> 00:00:23,766
like crazy,

17
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:25,846
and it's probably not the one

18
00:00:25,646 --> 00:00:26,246
you'd guess.

19
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:31,130
First,

20
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:32,790
a buyout is not a layoff.

21
00:00:32,590 --> 00:00:33,710
In a layoff,

22
00:00:33,510 --> 00:00:35,010
the company decides you're leaving.

23
00:00:35,591 --> 00:00:36,971
A buyout is an offer.

24
00:00:37,371 --> 00:00:38,791
The company pays you to leave

25
00:00:38,591 --> 00:00:39,291
voluntarily,

26
00:00:39,651 --> 00:00:41,371
usually with severance based on how

27
00:00:41,171 --> 00:00:42,231
long you've worked there.

28
00:00:42,871 --> 00:00:44,791
Long-time employees usually get the biggest

29
00:00:44,591 --> 00:00:45,091
checks.

30
00:00:45,896 --> 00:00:48,016
Microsoft's offer goes to staff at

31
00:00:47,816 --> 00:00:49,556
the vice president level and below

32
00:00:49,356 --> 00:00:50,216
in the US,

33
00:00:50,336 --> 00:00:50,776
Canada,

34
00:00:51,136 --> 00:00:51,336
India,

35
00:00:51,136 --> 00:00:51,936
and the UK,

36
00:00:52,376 --> 00:00:53,676
and they have until early December

37
00:00:53,476 --> 00:00:54,336
to decide.

38
00:00:54,976 --> 00:00:57,216
It's actually Microsoft's second buyout this

39
00:00:57,016 --> 00:00:57,436
year.

40
00:00:57,896 --> 00:00:58,556
According to reports,

41
00:00:58,736 --> 00:01:00,416
an earlier offer went to employees

42
00:01:00,216 --> 00:01:01,296
near retirement age,

43
00:01:01,576 --> 00:01:02,896
and more than 30% of the

44
00:01:02,696 --> 00:01:05,316
roughly 8,750 people who were eligible

45
00:01:05,116 --> 00:01:05,936
took it.

46
00:01:06,452 --> 00:01:08,552
Microsoft is spending heavily on artificial

47
00:01:08,352 --> 00:01:09,632
intelligence infrastructure,

48
00:01:10,272 --> 00:01:11,392
and says it wants to control

49
00:01:11,192 --> 00:01:12,652
headcount while avoiding layoffs.

50
00:01:13,452 --> 00:01:15,152
And here's a surprising detail.

51
00:01:15,572 --> 00:01:17,012
At other big tech companies with

52
00:01:16,812 --> 00:01:17,672
similar programs,

53
00:01:17,952 --> 00:01:19,632
only about 3 to 5% of

54
00:01:19,432 --> 00:01:20,352
people said yes.

55
00:01:20,872 --> 00:01:22,552
The downside for companies is that

56
00:01:22,352 --> 00:01:23,932
the people most likely to take

57
00:01:23,732 --> 00:01:25,272
the money are often the ones

58
00:01:25,072 --> 00:01:26,812
who can easily find another job.

59
00:01:28,117 --> 00:01:28,637
So,

60
00:01:28,777 --> 00:01:30,617
are layoffs really surging across America?

61
00:01:31,257 --> 00:01:32,337
This is the official count,

62
00:01:32,137 --> 00:01:33,897
from a monthly survey of about

63
00:01:33,697 --> 00:01:35,897
21,000 employers run by the Bureau

64
00:01:35,697 --> 00:01:36,697
of Labor Statistics.

65
00:01:37,631 --> 00:01:38,311
In August,

66
00:01:38,111 --> 00:01:40,551
US employers laid off or discharged

67
00:01:40,351 --> 00:01:42,971
about 1.64 million workers.

68
00:01:43,745 --> 00:01:44,885
That sounds like a lot,

69
00:01:44,685 --> 00:01:46,045
until you compare it.

70
00:01:46,425 --> 00:01:47,145
In 2019,

71
00:01:46,945 --> 00:01:48,465
before the pandemic,

72
00:01:48,645 --> 00:01:50,925
the average was about 1.82 million

73
00:01:50,725 --> 00:01:51,325
a month.

74
00:01:51,745 --> 00:01:52,865
As a share of all jobs,

75
00:01:53,085 --> 00:01:54,745
it's about 1% a month now,

76
00:01:54,945 --> 00:01:57,285
compared with 1.2% in 2019.

77
00:01:58,129 --> 00:01:59,629
And remember March 2020?

78
00:02:00,009 --> 00:02:01,829
Almost 13 million people were laid

79
00:02:01,629 --> 00:02:02,729
off in a single month.

80
00:02:02,849 --> 00:02:04,249
Today is nowhere near that.

81
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:07,710
Economists also watch a faster signal:

82
00:02:07,870 --> 00:02:09,690
new claims for unemployment benefits,

83
00:02:09,490 --> 00:02:11,150
reported every single week.

84
00:02:11,722 --> 00:02:12,462
Last week,

85
00:02:12,322 --> 00:02:14,822
about 194,000 people filed a new

86
00:02:14,622 --> 00:02:15,102
claim.

87
00:02:15,562 --> 00:02:16,102
In 2019,

88
00:02:15,942 --> 00:02:19,382
the weekly average was about 218,000.

89
00:02:19,925 --> 00:02:21,865
If mass layoffs were spreading across

90
00:02:21,665 --> 00:02:22,445
the country,

91
00:02:22,645 --> 00:02:24,165
this number would be shooting up.

92
00:02:24,265 --> 00:02:24,845
So far,

93
00:02:24,885 --> 00:02:25,545
it isn't.

94
00:02:25,967 --> 00:02:27,647
The unemployment rate says the same

95
00:02:27,447 --> 00:02:27,907
thing.

96
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:29,367
It's 4.2%,

97
00:02:29,167 --> 00:02:31,487
a little higher than the 3.7%

98
00:02:31,287 --> 00:02:32,327
average of 2019,

99
00:02:32,947 --> 00:02:34,887
but still low by historical standards.

100
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:37,750
To understand today,

101
00:02:37,790 --> 00:02:40,150
rewind to 2021 and 2022.

102
00:02:40,310 --> 00:02:41,450
After the pandemic,

103
00:02:41,470 --> 00:02:43,110
companies couldn't hire fast enough.

104
00:02:43,673 --> 00:02:45,513
Job openings hit record highs,

105
00:02:45,313 --> 00:02:46,873
and a record number of people

106
00:02:46,673 --> 00:02:47,793
quit for better pay.

107
00:02:48,413 --> 00:02:49,993
Some called it the great resignation.

108
00:02:50,484 --> 00:02:52,484
Then the Federal Reserve raised interest

109
00:02:52,284 --> 00:02:54,464
rates 11 times to fight inflation.

110
00:02:55,024 --> 00:02:56,164
Borrowing got expensive,

111
00:02:55,964 --> 00:02:57,444
companies slowed down,

112
00:02:57,244 --> 00:02:58,704
and hiring cooled off.

113
00:02:59,338 --> 00:03:01,198
That's exactly what higher rates are

114
00:03:00,998 --> 00:03:01,898
designed to do,

115
00:03:02,038 --> 00:03:03,258
and it's a big reason the

116
00:03:03,058 --> 00:03:04,898
job market feels so different today.

117
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:07,810
So if layoffs are low,

118
00:03:07,910 --> 00:03:09,250
why does the job market feel

119
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:09,830
so scary?

120
00:03:10,270 --> 00:03:12,090
Because the big change isn't firing.

121
00:03:12,370 --> 00:03:12,950
It's hiring.

122
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:15,030
In March 2022,

123
00:03:14,830 --> 00:03:18,090
there were about 12.3 million open

124
00:03:17,890 --> 00:03:19,110
jobs in America.

125
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:21,710
Now there are about 7.1 million.

126
00:03:22,381 --> 00:03:23,201
Back then,

127
00:03:23,381 --> 00:03:24,861
there were 2 open jobs for

128
00:03:24,661 --> 00:03:26,101
every unemployed person.

129
00:03:26,321 --> 00:03:27,541
Now there's about 1.

130
00:03:28,148 --> 00:03:29,748
And companies are hiring slowly.

131
00:03:30,328 --> 00:03:30,808
In August,

132
00:03:30,788 --> 00:03:32,868
only 3.3% of workers were new

133
00:03:32,668 --> 00:03:33,128
hires,

134
00:03:33,228 --> 00:03:35,608
compared with 3.9% in 2019.

135
00:03:36,264 --> 00:03:38,144
Economists call this a low-hire,

136
00:03:37,944 --> 00:03:39,284
low-fire job market.

137
00:03:39,604 --> 00:03:40,324
If you have a job,

138
00:03:40,424 --> 00:03:41,224
it's fairly safe.

139
00:03:41,644 --> 00:03:42,544
But if you lose one,

140
00:03:42,604 --> 00:03:44,084
finding the next one takes longer.

141
00:03:44,824 --> 00:03:46,744
The average unemployed person has now

142
00:03:46,544 --> 00:03:48,304
been looking for about 25 weeks,

143
00:03:48,464 --> 00:03:50,124
compared with about 22 weeks in

144
00:03:49,924 --> 00:03:50,664
2019.

145
00:03:52,117 --> 00:03:53,717
You can see the nervousness in

146
00:03:53,517 --> 00:03:54,337
another number:

147
00:03:54,377 --> 00:03:55,357
how many people quit.

148
00:03:55,917 --> 00:03:57,197
In April 2022,

149
00:03:56,997 --> 00:03:59,917
a record 4.5 million Americans quit

150
00:03:59,717 --> 00:04:01,217
their jobs in a single month.

151
00:04:01,770 --> 00:04:03,630
Now it's about 3.1 million.

152
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:04,870
People aren't leaving,

153
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,210
because they're not sure they'll find

154
00:04:06,010 --> 00:04:06,790
something better.

155
00:04:07,426 --> 00:04:08,766
Some people call it the great

156
00:04:08,566 --> 00:04:09,046
stay.

157
00:04:09,426 --> 00:04:11,646
Fewer people quitting also means fewer

158
00:04:11,446 --> 00:04:12,986
openings for everyone else.

159
00:04:14,383 --> 00:04:14,843
Now,

160
00:04:14,903 --> 00:04:15,803
the tech industry.

161
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:17,763
The government's information sector,

162
00:04:17,963 --> 00:04:19,383
which includes software publishers,

163
00:04:19,643 --> 00:04:19,963
telecom,

164
00:04:19,763 --> 00:04:20,803
and media,

165
00:04:21,003 --> 00:04:22,243
shows the pressure clearly.

166
00:04:22,938 --> 00:04:25,398
It peaked at about 3.12 million

167
00:04:25,198 --> 00:04:26,878
jobs in November 2022.

168
00:04:27,638 --> 00:04:29,718
Today it's about 2.74 million.

169
00:04:30,279 --> 00:04:33,139
That's about 376,000 fewer jobs,

170
00:04:33,259 --> 00:04:34,339
or roughly 12%.

171
00:04:35,007 --> 00:04:36,767
Many tech companies say they hired

172
00:04:36,567 --> 00:04:38,287
too fast during the pandemic,

173
00:04:38,547 --> 00:04:39,967
and now they're shifting money toward

174
00:04:39,767 --> 00:04:41,007
artificial intelligence,

175
00:04:40,807 --> 00:04:42,847
like the giant data centers Microsoft

176
00:04:42,647 --> 00:04:43,547
is building.

177
00:04:44,131 --> 00:04:45,991
So is AI replacing these workers?

178
00:04:46,631 --> 00:04:48,271
Microsoft says it is not replacing

179
00:04:48,071 --> 00:04:49,471
them with AI agents.

180
00:04:50,171 --> 00:04:51,471
But the pressure to cut costs

181
00:04:51,271 --> 00:04:53,331
while spending billions on AI is

182
00:04:53,131 --> 00:04:53,851
very real.

183
00:04:55,150 --> 00:04:56,510
So who is still hiring?

184
00:04:56,930 --> 00:04:57,410
First,

185
00:04:57,510 --> 00:04:58,310
the big picture.

186
00:04:58,929 --> 00:05:00,309
Over the past 12 months,

187
00:05:00,369 --> 00:05:03,589
the US economy added about 496,000

188
00:05:03,389 --> 00:05:04,269
jobs in total.

189
00:05:04,849 --> 00:05:06,429
That's about 41,000 a month.

190
00:05:06,829 --> 00:05:07,529
In 2019,

191
00:05:07,389 --> 00:05:10,349
it was about 165,000 a month.

192
00:05:11,015 --> 00:05:11,895
Now the surprise.

193
00:05:11,695 --> 00:05:15,095
Health care alone added about 520,000

194
00:05:14,895 --> 00:05:15,395
jobs.

195
00:05:15,795 --> 00:05:17,395
That's more than the entire economy

196
00:05:17,195 --> 00:05:18,015
added overall,

197
00:05:18,255 --> 00:05:20,095
which means many other industries actually

198
00:05:19,895 --> 00:05:20,515
shrank.

199
00:05:21,086 --> 00:05:23,746
The information sector lost about 120,000

200
00:05:23,546 --> 00:05:24,946
jobs over the same period,

201
00:05:24,746 --> 00:05:26,966
while manufacturing barely moved.

202
00:05:27,126 --> 00:05:27,686
Hospitals,

203
00:05:27,686 --> 00:05:28,046
clinics,

204
00:05:28,286 --> 00:05:29,606
and home health care are where

205
00:05:29,406 --> 00:05:30,286
the hiring is.

206
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:33,250
So how will you know if

207
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:34,290
things are really getting worse?

208
00:05:34,610 --> 00:05:35,510
Watch two numbers.

209
00:05:36,216 --> 00:05:38,496
The first is weekly jobless claims.

210
00:05:39,036 --> 00:05:41,016
It's about 194 thousand now.

211
00:05:41,516 --> 00:05:43,076
Many economists treat a steady climb

212
00:05:42,876 --> 00:05:44,556
toward 300 thousand as a warning

213
00:05:44,356 --> 00:05:44,896
sign.

214
00:05:45,566 --> 00:05:47,146
The second is the Sahm rule,

215
00:05:47,226 --> 00:05:49,226
a recession signal created by economist

216
00:05:49,026 --> 00:05:50,626
Claudia Sahm.

217
00:05:50,426 --> 00:05:52,206
It compares the recent unemployment rate

218
00:05:52,006 --> 00:05:53,346
with its low over the past

219
00:05:53,146 --> 00:05:53,626
year.

220
00:05:53,986 --> 00:05:54,566
Right now,

221
00:05:54,566 --> 00:05:55,986
it reads 0.00.

222
00:05:56,775 --> 00:05:58,455
When it reaches 0.5,

223
00:05:58,555 --> 00:06:00,515
a recession has usually already started.

224
00:06:01,075 --> 00:06:01,275
Today,

225
00:06:01,255 --> 00:06:02,055
it's nowhere close.

226
00:06:03,383 --> 00:06:05,003
So what does this mean for

227
00:06:04,803 --> 00:06:05,183
you?

228
00:06:05,323 --> 00:06:06,663
Here's what the data suggests.

229
00:06:07,412 --> 00:06:07,892
One:

230
00:06:07,692 --> 00:06:09,092
in a low-hire market,

231
00:06:09,192 --> 00:06:10,912
an emergency fund matters more,

232
00:06:10,712 --> 00:06:12,272
because a job search can take

233
00:06:12,072 --> 00:06:12,532
longer.

234
00:06:13,212 --> 00:06:15,192
Many experts suggest three to six

235
00:06:14,992 --> 00:06:16,152
months of expenses.

236
00:06:17,005 --> 00:06:17,485
Two:

237
00:06:17,765 --> 00:06:19,105
if you're thinking about quitting,

238
00:06:19,265 --> 00:06:20,725
line up the next job first.

239
00:06:21,438 --> 00:06:21,918
Three:

240
00:06:22,098 --> 00:06:23,158
keep your skills current.

241
00:06:23,818 --> 00:06:25,298
Learning to use AI tools in

242
00:06:25,098 --> 00:06:26,458
your own field can make you

243
00:06:26,258 --> 00:06:26,938
more valuable,

244
00:06:27,178 --> 00:06:27,698
not less.

245
00:06:28,379 --> 00:06:29,099
And four:

246
00:06:29,159 --> 00:06:30,599
if you're ever offered a buyout,

247
00:06:30,559 --> 00:06:31,999
compare the severance with how long

248
00:06:31,799 --> 00:06:33,159
a job search might take in

249
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:33,519
your field,

250
00:06:33,319 --> 00:06:34,939
and talk to a professional before

251
00:06:34,739 --> 00:06:35,359
you decide.

252
00:06:36,030 --> 00:06:36,750
And five:

253
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:37,850
if you're ever laid off,

254
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:39,790
file for unemployment benefits right away.

255
00:06:40,010 --> 00:06:41,190
Rules differ by state,

256
00:06:41,250 --> 00:06:43,050
and taking a voluntary buyout can

257
00:06:42,850 --> 00:06:44,070
affect whether you qualify,

258
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:45,550
so check with your state first.

259
00:06:46,950 --> 00:06:47,490
So,

260
00:06:47,670 --> 00:06:48,970
layoffs are not surging.

261
00:06:49,370 --> 00:06:50,650
The real story is a job

262
00:06:50,450 --> 00:06:52,270
market where hiring has slowed way

263
00:06:52,070 --> 00:06:52,630
down,

264
00:06:52,430 --> 00:06:53,870
and tech is feeling it the

265
00:06:53,670 --> 00:06:54,110
most.

266
00:06:54,717 --> 00:06:56,577
Microsoft's buyout is a sign of

267
00:06:56,377 --> 00:06:56,997
the times:

268
00:06:57,617 --> 00:06:59,477
big companies are pouring money into

269
00:06:59,277 --> 00:07:01,617
AI and trimming almost everywhere else.

270
00:07:02,268 --> 00:07:03,128
Next time:

271
00:07:02,928 --> 00:07:04,868
how banks create money out of

272
00:07:04,668 --> 00:07:05,308
thin air.

273
00:07:05,768 --> 00:07:07,208
Subscribe to Coin Theory so you

274
00:07:07,008 --> 00:07:07,848
don't miss it.

